# /etc/cron.d file — needs the user field (5th column) since it's system-wide,
# not a per-user crontab. Logs to /var/log/wpdev/cron.log (bind-mounted from
# ./logs/ on the host, same place nginx's logs live) — NOT to PID 1's
# stdout/stderr: php-fpm internally remaps its own fd 1/2 to /dev/null some
# time after its startup banner, so anything written there after that point
# never reaches `docker logs`, discovered the hard way while building this.
#
# PATH must be set explicitly: cron's own default PATH (just /usr/bin:/bin)
# doesn't include /usr/local/bin, so without this, not just `wp` but even
# the wp phar's own `#!/usr/bin/env php` shebang fails to resolve — php
# itself lives in /usr/local/bin in this image.
# Runs as www-data -- which the entrypoint renumbers to your own uid -- so
# anything a cron event writes is owned by you, same as PHP-FPM's files.
#
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
* * * * * www-data /usr/local/bin/wp-cron-runner.sh >> /var/log/wpdev/cron.log 2>&1

